<section xml:id="sec-conditional-statements">
	<title>Conditional Gates</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			So far, we've written code that runs steps in sequence. But real programs 
			often need to make decisions, choosing different actions based on conditions. 
			This is called flow control. In MATLAB, you use things like <pf>if</pf>-statements or 
			<pf>switch</pf>-statements to guide your code down the right path.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<subsection xml:id="sec-if-statement">
		<title>If Statement</title>

		<p>
			The simplest <pf>if</pf>-statement has one condition and one block of code. The code runs only when the
			condition is true.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="single-if-statement-structure">
			<title>The if Statement Structure</title>

			<sidebyside widths="15% 85%">
				<p>Use Case: </p>
				<p>if <pf>condition</pf> is true, run <pf>codeBlock</pf>.</p>
			</sidebyside>

			<program language="matlab" line-numbers="no">
				if condition     % true? ➜ run codeBlock
					codeBlock
				end
			</program>

			<p>
				Notes &amp; Rules:
				<ul>
					<li>Every <pf>if</pf>-statement must end with <pf>end</pf>.</li>
					<li><pf>condition</pf> must be a logical statement that returns <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.</li>
					<li>If <pf>condition</pf> is false, MATLAB does nothing and skips to <pf>end</pf>.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<p>
			To see how this works, run the following code block:
		</p>

		<sage language="octave">
			<input>
				age = 23;

				if age &gt;= 21
					fprintf("Bouncer: You may enter the bar.\n");
				end
			</input>
		</sage>

		<p>
			Since <pf>age</pf> is <pf>23</pf>, the logical statement <pf>age &gt;= 21</pf> 
			is true, so MATLAB executes the <pf>fprintf</pf> line and prints the message. 
			Now try changing <pf>age</pf> to <pf>16</pf> and running it again. This time, 
			the <pf>age &gt;= 21</pf> is false, so MATLAB skips the <pf>fprintf</pf> line 
			and nothing is printed.
		</p>

	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="sec-if-else-statement">
		<title>If-else Statement</title>

		<p>
			When you have more than one possibility, you can add an <pf>else</pf> branch. MATLAB checks the <pf>if</pf> condition;
			if it is true, it executes the <pf>if</pf> branch; if it is false, it moves directly into the <pf>else</pf> branch.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="if-else-structure">
			<title>The if-else Statement Structure</title>

			<sidebyside widths="15% 85%">
				<p>Use Case: </p>
				<p>if <pf>conditionA</pf> is true, run <pf>blockA</pf>. Otherwise, run <pf>blockB</pf>. </p>
			</sidebyside>

			<program language="matlab" line-numbers="no">
				if conditionA     % true? ➜ run blockA -- false? ➜ run blockB
					blockA			
				else
					blockB
				end
			</program>

			<p>
				Notes &amp; Rules:
				<ul>
					<li>Every <pf>if</pf>-statement must end with <pf>end</pf>.</li>
					<li><pf>conditionA</pf> must be a logical statement that returns <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.</li>
					<li>Exactly one of <pf>blockA</pf> OR <pf>blockB</pf> must run when the code executes.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<p>
			Building on the previous example, here is a code block that uses an <pf>if-else</pf> statement:
		</p>

		<sage language="octave">
			<input>
				age = 23;

				if age &gt;= 21
					fprintf("Bouncer: You may enter the bar.\n");
				else
					fprintf("Bouncer: Move along kid.\n");
				end
			</input>
		</sage>

		<p>
			Since <pf>age</pf> is <pf>23</pf>, the logical statement <pf>age &gt;= 21</pf> is true, so MATLAB prints the first message. Now try changing <pf>age</pf> to <pf>16</pf> and running it again. This time, the logical statement is false, so MATLAB tells you to move along.
		</p>

	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="sec-if-elseif-else-statement">
		<title>If-elseif-else Statement</title>

		<p>
			An <pf>else</pf> branch acts as a fallback: it runs only if every earlier condition is false.
			This is useful when you want to guarantee that some variable gets assigned a value.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="if-elseif-else-statement-structure">
			<title>The if-elseif-else Statement Structure</title>

			<sidebyside widths="15% 85%">
				<p>Use Case: </p>
				<p>
					<ul>
						<li>if <pf>conditionA</pf> is true, run <pf>blockA</pf>,</li>
						<li>if <pf>conditionB</pf> is true, run <pf>blockB</pf>,</li>
						<li>if <pf>conditionC</pf> is true, run <pf>blockC</pf>,</li>
						<li>if all of the above are false, run <pf>blockD</pf> (default).</li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</sidebyside>

				<program language="matlab" line-numbers="no">
					if conditionA     % true? ➜ run blockA -- false? ➜ check conditionB
						blockA			
					elseif conditionB % true? ➜ run blockB -- false? ➜ check conditionC
						blockB
					elseif conditionC % true? ➜ run blockC -- false? ➜ run blockD
						blockC
					else
						blockD
					end
				</program>

				<p>
					Notes &amp; Rules:
					<ul>
						<li>You can add as many <pf>elseif</pf> branches as needed.</li>
						<li>The <pf>else</pf> (default) branch is optional.</li>
						<li>The conditions are checked in order from top to bottom.</li>
						<li>Exactly one of the blocks will run when the code executes.</li>
						<li>
							Every <pf>if</pf>-statement must end with <pf>end</pf>.
						</li>
						<li>
							Each <pf>logicalStatement</pf> must evaluate to <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.
						</li>
						<li>
							The <pf>else</pf> block runs only when all preceding conditions are false.
						</li>
						<li>
							You can nest <pf>if</pf>-statements within other <pf>if</pf>-statements, but this should be avoided whenever possible.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</p>

		</assemblage>

		<sage language="octave">
			<input>
				x = 3;
				y = 2;

				if y ~= 3
					z = 4;
				elseif x &lt; y
					z = 3;
				else
					z = 0;
				end

				fprintf("The value of z is: %d\n", z);
			</input>
		</sage>

		<p>
			For practice, test this block with different initial values such as
			<pf>x = 0; y = 4;</pf>, <pf>x = 0; y = 3;</pf>, and <pf>x = 3; y = 3;</pf>.
			Watch how only one branch executes each time.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="sec-switch-statement">
		<title>Switch Statement</title>
		
		<p>
			The switch statement is another option for controlling code in MATLAB. It works similarly to the if-statement by directing your code to go down one of many possible paths.
		</p>

		<p>
			The switch statement determines what code to execute based on possible values of some variable. The conditions we use with switch statements are in the form of cases. 
		</p>

		<aside>
			<p>
				Like the if-statement, you can have as many branches as you like. You would just add additional <pf>case</pf> statements between the <pf>switch</pf> and <pf>otherwise</pf> statements.
			</p>
		</aside>

		<assemblage xml:id="switch-statement-def">
			<title>The switch Statement Structure</title>

			<sidebyside widths="15% 85%">
				<p>Use Case: </p>
				<p>
					<ul>
						<li>if <pf>var</pf> is equal to <pf>val1</pf>, run <pf>blockA</pf>,</li>
						<li>if <pf>var</pf> is equal to <pf>val2</pf> or <pf>val3</pf>, run <pf>blockB</pf>,</li>
						<li>if <pf>var</pf> is equal to <pf>val4</pf>, run <pf>blockC</pf>,</li>
						<li>if <pf>var</pf> was not matched, run <pf>blockD</pf> (default).</li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</sidebyside>

			<program language="matlab">
				switch var

					case val1          % var == val1? YES ➜ run blockA 
						               %              NO  ➜ check next case
						blockA
					case {val2, val3}  % var == val2 OR val3? Yes ➜ run blockB
						               %                      NO  ➜ check next case
						blockB
					case val4          % var == val4? Yes ➜ run blockC
						               %              NO  ➜ check next case
						blockC
					otherwise          % no matches? YES ➜ run blockD
						               %             NO  ➜ check next case
						blockD
				end
			</program>

			<p>
				Rules:
				<ul>
					<li>Case values are checked in order from top to bottom.</li>
					<li>The <pf>otherwise</pf> block runs only when no cases match.</li>
					<li>The <pf>otherwise</pf> (default) branch is optional.</li>
					<li>When there is only one case value, curly braces <pf>{ }</pf> are optional.</li>
					<li>Every <pf>switch</pf>-statement must end with <pf>end</pf>.</li>
					<li>Case values can be numbers, strings, or other data types that support equality comparisons.</li>
					<li>Case values are case-sensitive when comparing strings.</li>
					<li>Case values must be unique; duplicate case values will cause an error.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>
				Limitations:
				<ul>
					<li>Case values cannot be variables. They are known hard-coded values.</li>
					<li>Case values cannot use logical operators (e.g., <pf>&lt;</pf>, <pf>&gt;</pf>, <pf>==</pf>, <pf>&amp;</pf>, <pf>|</pf>).</li>
					<li>Switch statements are not suitable for range-based conditions (e.g., checking if a value is between two numbers).</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<p>
			The <pf>switch</pf>-<pf>case</pf> structure is similar to the <pf>if</pf> structure, but offers a few advantages.	First, it is easier to read; second, it is better when comparing strings (of possibly different lengths). The basic format is:
		</p>

		<p>
			Let's check whether a cadet is in their first two years at VMI.
		</p>

		<sage language="octave">
			<input>
				year = 'second class';

				switch year
					case{'fourth class', 'third class'}
						fprintf("You are in the first two years.\n");
					case{'second class', 'first class'}
						fprintf("You are in the last two years.\n");
					otherwise
						fprintf("You must be a fifth year.\n");
				end
			</input>
		</sage>

		<p>
			The cases are grouped by curly brackets so that a case will be satisfied if the value of Year is any of the values in a specific case. Once this code is executed, the switch command will look at the value of Year, and the output should be
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			<code>
				You are in your last two years.
			</code>
		</program>

	</subsection>

	<reading-questions>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				The examples below emphasize two goals: translating English conditions into logical statements, and writing
				conditions that are readable.
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<exercise xml:id="if-activity-1">
			<title>Activity 1: Simple <pf>if</pf>-statement</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Use the coding area, below, to complete the following tasks. Each task builds on the previous one, so you can modify your code block as you go.
				</p>

				<sage language="octave">
					<input>
						% ---- Type your code here ----
						

						% -----------------------------
						fprintf('for x = %d, we set y to %d\n', x, y);
					</input>
				</sage>
			</introduction>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Define the variable <pf>x</pf> as <pf>6</pf>, if use an <pf>if</pf>-statement to define the variable <pf>y</pf> according to the following rule:
						<ul marker="">
							<li>if <pf>x</pf> is less than or equal to 9, set <pf>y</pf> to 1, otherwise set <pf>y</pf> to 4.</li>
						</ul>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = 6;

						if x &lt;= 9
							y = 1;
						else
							y = 4;
						end

						fprintf('for x = %d, we set y to %d\n', x, y);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Change <pf>x</pf> to <pf>16</pf> and rerun your code block.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>Just change the value of <pf>x</pf> to <pf>16</pf> in the previous code block.</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Next, define <pf>x</pf> as a random integer between -5 and 15, using the command <pf>randi([-5, 15])</pf>.
					</p>
					<p>
						Rerun your code multiple times to see how the value of <pf>y</pf> changes based on the random value of <pf>x</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>Just change the value of <pf>x</pf> to <pf>randi([-5, 15])</pf> in the previous code block.</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Update your code to set <pf>y</pf> according to the following new rule:
						<ul marker="">
							<li>if <pf>x</pf> is between 1 and 9 (inclusive), set <pf>y</pf> to 1, otherwise set <pf>y</pf> to 4.</li>
						</ul>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = randi([-5, 15]);

						if x &gt;= 1 &amp; x &lt;= 9
							y = 1;
						else
							y = 4;
						end

						fprintf('for x = %d, we set y to %d\n', x, y);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<assemblage>
			<p>
				<exercise xml:id="if-activity-2">
					<title>Activity 2: Simple <pf>if</pf>-statement</title>

					<statement>
						<p>
							Use the coding area, below, to set <pf>w</pf> according to the following rules:
							<ul>
								<li>if <pf>x</pf> is equal to 9, set <pf>w</pf> to 1</li>
								<li>if <pf>x</pf> is between (but not equal to) -4 and 0, set <pf>w</pf> to 2</li>
								<li>if <pf>x</pf> is not less than 3 or greater than 9, set <pf>w</pf> to 3</li>
								<li>if none of the above are true, set <pf>w</pf> to 4</li>
							</ul>
						</p>

						<sage language="octave">
							<input>
								x = randi([-5, 15]);
								% ---- Type your code here ----
								

								% -----------------------------
								fprintf('for x = %d, we set w to %d\n', x, w);
							</input>
						</sage>

					</statement>

					<solution>
						
						<program language="matlab">
							x = randi([-5, 15]);

							if x == 9
								w = 1;
							elseif x &gt; -4 &amp;&amp; x &lt; 0
								w = 2;
							elseif ~(x &lt;= 3) || x &gt; 9
								w = 3;
							else
								w = 4;
							end

							fprintf('for x = %d, we set w to %d\n', x, w);
						</program>
					</solution>

				</exercise>
			</p>
		</assemblage>

		<exercise xml:id="if-activity-3">
			<title>Activity 3</title>
			
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Use the coding area, below, to complete the following tasks.
				</p>

				<sage language="octave">
					<input>
						num = randi([0, 20]);
						fprintf('Starting num = %d\n', num);
						% ---- Type your code here ----
						

						% -----------------------------
						fprintf('Updated num = %d\n', num);
					</input>
				</sage>
			</introduction>

			<task xml:id="f-activity-4">

				<statement>
					<p>
						Suppose <pf>num</pf> is a random integer between 0 and 20. Give the code that increases <pf>num</pf> by 5 if it is greater than 10 and decreases it by 5 otherwise.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						<code>
							nnum = randi([0, 20]);
							fprintf('Starting num = %d\n', num);

							if num &gt; 10
								num = num + 5;
							else
								num = num - 5;
							end

							fprintf('Updated num = %d\n', num);
						</code>
					</program>
				</solution>

			</task>

			<task xml:id="f-activity-5">

				<statement>
					<p>
						Suppose <pf>num</pf> is a <pf>1 x 1 double</pf>. Give the code that increases <pf>num</pf> by 5 if it is greater than 10, decreases <pf>num</pf> by 5 if it is less than 10, and does nothing if <pf>num</pf> is zero.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						If <pf>x = 0</pf>, the below script runs the commands <pf>x = x - 5</pf> and <pf>disp(x)</pf>, outputting <pf>-5</pf>. However, if <pf>x = 10</pf>, only the <pf>disp(x)</pf> command is run, producing an output of <pf>10</pf>.
					</p>

					<program language="matlab">
						<code>
							if x &gt; 10
								x = x + 5;
							elseif x &lt; 10
								x = x - 5;
							end
							disp(x)
						</code>
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="sec-quadratic-equation">
			<title>Activity 4: Solving the Quadratic Equation in MATLAB</title>

			<introduction>
			<p>
				A classic programming task is turning a familiar mathematical procedure into a reliable piece of code.
				The quadratic equation is a great example because it combines algebra, branching logic, and careful handling
				of special cases.
			</p>

			<p>
				Consider the quadratic polynomial <m>a x^2 + b x + c</m>. Its roots satisfy
				<me>x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.</me>
				The expression <m>D = b^2 - 4ac</m> is called the <term>discriminant</term>, and its sign determines
				the type of roots.
			</p>
			</introduction>

			<task xml:id="subsec-quadratic-discriminant">
				<title>The Discriminant and Root Types</title>

				<p>
					There are three common cases:
					if <m>D &gt; 0</m> there are two distinct real roots, if <m>D = 0</m> there is one repeated real root,
					and if <m>D &lt; 0</m> the roots are complex conjugates.
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
		a = 1; b = -3; c = 2;        % (x-1)(x-2) = 0

		D = b^2 - 4*a*c;

		x1 = (-b + sqrt(D)) / (2*a);
		x2 = (-b - sqrt(D)) / (2*a);
				</program>

				<p>
					MATLAB’s <code>sqrt</code> can produce complex results automatically when <m>D &lt; 0</m>,
					so the computation above still works. But it is often useful to detect the case and present results clearly.
				</p>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="subsec-quadratic-function">
				<title>Packaging the Computation as a Function</title>

				<p>
					A function is the natural way to turn this idea into reusable code.
					We also add input checks to catch the invalid case <m>a = 0</m>, which would not be a quadratic.
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
		function [r1, r2] = quadraticRoots(a,b,c)
		%QUADRATICROOTS Return the roots of a*x^2 + b*x + c.
		%
		%   r1, r2 are the two roots (possibly equal or complex).

				if a == 0
						error("Input a must be nonzero for a quadratic polynomial.");
				end

				D = b^2 - 4*a*c;
				r1 = (-b + sqrt(D)) / (2*a);
				r2 = (-b - sqrt(D)) / (2*a);
		end
				</program>

				<p>
					A function like this becomes more valuable when paired with clear documentation and predictable output format.
					In practice, you might also return the discriminant or a label indicating the root type.
				</p>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="subsec-quadratic-numerics">
				<title>A Numerical Note</title>

				<p>
					For some coefficient values, the quadratic formula can suffer from roundoff error.
					This usually appears when <m>b^2</m> is much larger than <m>4ac</m>, causing subtractive cancellation.
					For introductory programming, the standard formula is perfectly acceptable, but it is good to know that
					numerical stability can matter even in “simple” algebra.
				</p>
			</task>
		</exercise>

	</reading-questions>

